{:up ["CREATE TABLE scopes (
         id          INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
         title       TEXT NOT NULL,
         description TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
         user_id     INTEGER REFERENCES users(id),
         UNIQUE(title, user_id)
       )"
      "CREATE TABLE recipe_scopes (
         recipe_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES recipes(id),
         scope_id  INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES scopes(id),
         PRIMARY KEY (recipe_id, scope_id)
       )"]
 ;; **Scope**: a title and a description a Recipe can be filed under, any number
 ;; of them to a Recipe. Cookbook's own word, and from here on its only meaning —
 ;; the visibility argument the db layer used to call `scope` is now `audience`
 ;; (see the rename commit before this one, and `db.recipe/audience-clause`).
 ;;
 ;; The shape follows tracker's categories — its
 ;; `007-fix-category-unique-per-user` for the entity and
 ;; `018-add-resource-categories` for the join — with two departures, both
 ;; deliberate.
 ;;
 ;; **`title`, not tracker's `name`.** Cookbook's own entity calls that field
 ;; `title`, and so did the owner when he asked for this. One app, one word for
 ;; the thing a row is called by.
 ;;
 ;; **No `category_type` column.** Tracker's join table carries one because it has
 ;; four kinds of category sharing a table; cookbook has one kind, so the
 ;; discriminator would have exactly one possible value. A column that can only
 ;; ever hold one answer answers nothing, and it would have to be threaded through
 ;; every query and every insert to keep saying it.
 ;;
 ;; `UNIQUE(title, user_id)` and not `UNIQUE(title)`: a global uniqueness
 ;; constraint on a per-user entity is the bug tracker's migration 007 exists to
 ;; fix, so this starts where tracker ended up. `user_id` is nullable for the same
 ;; reason `recipes.user_id` is — dev's owner has no `users` row, and
 ;; `db/user-id-where-clause` reads a missing id as `user_id IS NULL`, which is a
 ;; real owner in this schema. SQLite treats NULLs as distinct in a UNIQUE index,
 ;; so the constraint does not bind the nil owner's rows; that is the pre-existing
 ;; shape of every table here and not a decision made now.
 ;;
 ;; **The `REFERENCES` clauses are documentation, not enforcement.** `PRAGMA
 ;; foreign_keys` is 0 on this connection and nothing in `src/` or the JDBC URLs
 ;; turns it on, so no cascade fires and no orphan is refused. They are declared
 ;; anyway, matching the house style — every other table here says what it points
 ;; at — but the code does the work: `db.recipe/delete-recipe` deletes a Recipe's
 ;; `recipe_scopes` rows in its own transaction, the way it already deletes its
 ;; `recipe_history` rows, and `db.scope/delete-scope` deletes the join rows of
 ;; the Scope going away. Both are pinned by a test that reads the join table
 ;; afterwards rather than only checking the parent row is gone.
 ;;
 ;; **The join table carries no `user_id`.** Both of its parents have one, and an
 ;; association only ever links a Recipe and a Scope with the same owner —
 ;; `db.scope/set-recipe-scopes!` intersects the incoming ids with the caller's
 ;; own — so a third copy of that fact could only ever be the one that goes stale.
 ;; Reads still join through `scopes` and narrow on its `user_id`, so the
 ;; ownership question is answered from the row that owns it.
 :down ["DROP TABLE IF EXISTS recipe_scopes"
        "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS scopes"]}
